sections.s2_technical:
Let me explain this calmly, because it is one of those features that looks exotic but solves a very practical problem.

A normal entity maps to one table via `@Table`. But sometimes one logical record is physically spread across two tables that share the same primary key. `@SecondaryTable` tells Hibernate about that second table and how it joins to the first.

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")                              // primary table
@SecondaryTable(
    name = "account_kyc",                              // secondary table
    pkJoinColumns = @PrimaryKeyJoinColumn(name = "account_id")  // joined on the shared PK
)
public class Account {
    @Id
    @Column(name = "account_id")
    private String accountId;                           // lives in BOTH tables as the PK

    @Column(name = "holder_name")
    private String holderName;                          // primary table (default)

    @Column(name = "balance")
    private java.math.BigDecimal balance;               // primary table (default)

    @Column(table = "account_kyc", name = "pan")
    private String pan;                                 // routed to the SECONDARY table

    @Column(table = "account_kyc", name = "aadhaar_doc", columnDefinition = "TEXT")
    private String aadhaarDoc;                          // large/cold column in secondary table
}
```

The key mechanics. The primary key column (`account_id`) exists in BOTH tables; the secondary table's primary key column is also a foreign key back to the primary table via `@PrimaryKeyJoinColumn`. Any field annotated `@Column(table = "account_kyc")` is stored in the secondary table; fields without a `table` attribute default to the primary table. On a read, Hibernate generates a JOIN between the two tables on the shared key and assembles one `Account`. On a save, Hibernate issues an INSERT or UPDATE against each table.

When I reach for this in practice. First, LEGACY schemas where a record was historically split across two tables and I want to model it as one clean entity rather than two entities with a one-to-one relationship. Second, separating HOT and COLD columns: keep the frequently-read columns (name, balance) in a narrow primary table for fast scans, and push rarely-read bulky columns (KYC documents, long notes) into a secondary table so the common queries do not drag them along. Third, isolating SENSITIVE data (PAN, Aadhaar) into its own table that can have stricter access controls, while still presenting a single entity to the code.

A performance nuance worth knowing: by default Hibernate joins the secondary table eagerly, so even a query that only needs name and balance may still join `account_kyc`. If the secondary columns are genuinely cold, that is wasteful. The cleaner alternative in that case is often a true `@OneToOne` association with lazy loading, so the secondary data loads only when touched. So I weigh `@SecondaryTable` (one entity, always joined, simplest model) against a lazy `@OneToOne` (two entities, load-on-demand, better when the secondary data is rarely needed). I also make sure the secondary table columns are nullable or that a row always exists, because a missing secondary row can produce nulls on an inner-vs-outer-join mismatch.

A senior point: `@SecondaryTable` is a mapping convenience, not a sharding or scaling tool. It does not split load across databases; both tables live in the same database and are joined by the same transaction. For genuine scaling you look at partitioning or separate services, not secondary tables.

